									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,2129674)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-2129674">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/ensimismada" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1451590); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1451590&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607958832" alt="ensimismada" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1451590)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/ensimismada" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1451590); return false;" >ensimismada</a></span> <span class="odate time_1413070312 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">11 Oct 2014 23:31</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2129674">
									

<p>I still am not engaged by the story. I did finish reading through this entire tale, though, so credit for that. I often will walk away 20% through a story if I'm not engaged.</p>
<p>Kalinin expressed concerns with the Foundation that I share. I was particularly bothered with the scene with poking the spider-plant with a stick and casually commenting on its composition while a team member is still in danger and while someone could be poisoned. There's little sense of what the Foundation is doing for containment, whether be of anomalous organisms or, especially, information about their existence.</p>
<p>New guy is definitely not freaking out enough. Or you could run with the incredulousness most scientists would express when confronted with things so entirely outside the realm of biological possibility. There's a bit of telling about his excitement instead showing it, but that's quickly forgotten.</p>
<p>Finally, I felt no change in pace whatsoever during the action sequence involving the spider bite. There was no sense that anything was at risk and there was no feeling of frenzy, of characters being rushed or confused. This might be aided by mixing up sentence structure and paying careful attention to diction. Action scenes are intensely sensory - quick flashes of images, sounds, or feeling in rapid succession - so shorter sentences convey this feel more efficiently.</p>
<p>No vote. I did fix a grammar error though.</p>

							</div>

								
					
